<!-- puka — a SELECTING RACE over the flows its block opens. The first flow through the opening
     yields the block's result; the rest run on, and only the enclosing scope's exit cancels them:

         <<~ puka>>
           <<~ lele "lar:///target-a">>
           <<~ lele "lar:///target-b">>
         <<~/puka>>

     A //puka// marks where passage happens. The fish through the gap decides the outcome; the net
     stays open behind it. `holo` keeps the other discipline — there the winner cancels the losers
     at once. `rush` mirrors this head in English and delegates here.

     A puka block stands OUTSIDE a `huli` iteration body — its law forbids the direct nesting.

     THE PAGE STAYS CLEAR. Coordination governs execution and never layout, so the definition hands
     the compile layer a data annotation naming the discipline and renders nothing. -->

\procedure ~puka()
<span class="lar-puka" data-losers="continue-until-scope-exit" hidden="true"/>
\end
